<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="coumarins biosynthesis (engineered)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-7398" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: Coumarins Coumarins| are a major group of plant natural products derived from the phenylpropanoid pathway. At least 1000 natural occurring coumarins, among them about 300 simple coumarins, have been found in many families of higher plants |CITS: [BERENBAUM91]|. Coumarins and their derivatives are used in many therapeutic applications including anti-bacterial agents, anti-inflammation, central nervous system stimulants, and anti-coagulants. Synthetic derivatives of 4-hydroxycoumarin such as warfarin, phenprocoumon, and acenocoumarol are among the most widely prescribed anticoagulant pharmaceuticals for the prevention and treatment of thrombosis and embolism. In addition, coumarins may have therapeutic roles in the treatment of cancers and AIDS |CITS: [15579072][17497014][16570501][18991629]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; This pathway provides the &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; biosynthesis in |FRAME: TAX-562 &lt;i&gt;Escherichia coli&lt;/i&gt;| of the simple coumarins |FRAME: SCOPOLETIN scopoletin|, |FRAME: CPD-8186 umbelliferone|, and |FRAME: CPD-8097 esculetin| by connecting the coumarin formation reactions with the bacterial primary metabolism. In plants coumarins are derived from |FRAME: PHE L-phenylalanine| (see |FRAME: PWY1F-467 phenylpropanoid biosynthesis, initial reactions|). In that pathway, |FRAME: COUMARATE 4-coumarate| is formed from |FRAME: PHE L-phenylalanine| in two steps, catalyzed by |FRAME: EC-4.3.1.25 EC 4.3.1.25, phenylalanine/tyrosine ammonia-lyase| and |FRAME: EC-1.14.14.91 EC 1.14.13.11, &lt;em&gt;trans&lt;/em&gt;-cinnamate 4-monooxygenase|, which is a cytochorme P-450 hydroxylase. Since the functional expression of plant P-450 enzymes is always difficult in bacterial systems due to their requirement for anchorage on endoplasmic reticulum and redox partners, this engineered pathway (described in |CITS: [21320631]|) starts with the enzyme |FRAME: EC-4.3.1.23 EC 4.3.1.23, tyrosine ammonia-lyase|, which converts |FRAME: TYR L-tyrosine| to |FRAME: COUMARATE 4-coumarate| in a single step. The enzyme that was selected for this pathway is from the red yeast |FRAME: TAX-5535 &lt;i&gt;Rhodotorula glutinis&lt;/i&gt;|, since it has a high activity with L-tyrosine |CITS: [17204442]|. The addition of the plant enzymes |FRAME: EC-6.2.1.12 EC 6.2.1.12, 4-coumarate&amp;mdash;CoA ligase| and |FRAME: MONOMER-18295 4-coumaroyl 2&apos;-hydroxylase| enable the synthesis of |FRAME: CPD-8186 umbelliferone| |CITS: [22168819]|. The synthesis of |FRAME: SCOPOLETIN scopoletin| and |FRAME: CPD-8097 esculetin| involves a second challenge regarding the formation of the intermediate |FRAME: CAFFEOYL-COA &lt;i&gt;trans&lt;/i&gt;-caffeoyl-CoA|. In plants, |FRAME: CAFFEOYL-COA &lt;i&gt;trans&lt;/i&gt;-caffeoyl-CoA| is produced from |FRAME: COUMARATE 4-coumarate| by two enzymes, one of which, |FRAME: EC-1.14.14.96 EC 1.14.13.36, 5-&lt;em&gt;O&lt;/em&gt;-(4-coumaroyl)-&lt;small&gt;D&lt;/small&gt;-quinate 3&amp;prime;-monooxygenase|, is another P-450 enzyme (see |FRAME: PWY-361 phenylpropanoid biosynthesis|). To bypass that enzyme, this pathway utilizes the bacterial enzyme |FRAME: EC-1.14.14.9 EC 1.14.14.9, 4-hydroxyphenylacetate 3-monooxygenase| (encoded in |FRAME: TAX-566546 &lt;i&gt;Escherichia coli&lt;/i&gt; W| by the |FRAME: G-2001 hpaB| gene) that can catalyze the hydroxylation of |FRAME: COUMARATE 4-coumarate| to |FRAME: CPD-676 &lt;i&gt;trans&lt;/i&gt;-caffeate| |CITS: [22475509]|, which is converted to |FRAME: CAFFEOYL-COA &lt;i&gt;trans&lt;/i&gt;-caffeoyl-CoA| by the plant enzyme |FRAME: EC-6.2.1.12 EC 6.2.1.12, 4-coumarate&amp;mdash;CoA ligase|. Once |FRAME: CAFFEOYL-COA &lt;i&gt;trans&lt;/i&gt;-caffeoyl-CoA| is formed, |FRAME: CPD-8097 esculetin| can be produced by including the plant enzyme |FRAME: AT3G13610-MONOMER feruloyl-CoA 6&apos;-hydroxylase| |CITS: [25927349]|, and |FRAME: SCOPOLETIN scopoletin| is produced by adding the plant enzyme |FRAME: EC-2.1.1.104 EC 2.1.1.104, caffeoyl-CoA &lt;em&gt;O&lt;/em&gt;-methyltransferase| |CITS: [18547395]|. It should be notes that the final conversion in each case involves a &lt;i&gt;trans-cis&lt;/i&gt; isomerization followed by a subsequent lactonization to close the side chain. The exact mechanism is unknown, and while no enzyme has been identified, the reaction is likely to proceed spontaneously |CITS: [Hartley75][Borges89]|.</Description>
