    <DataNode elementId="FAD" textLabel="FAD" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="IMGVNJNCCGXBHD-UYBVJOGSSA-K" dataSource="InChIKey" />
      <Graphics centerX="650" centerY="960" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment>|FRAME: FAD &quot;Flavin adenine dinucleotide&quot;| (FAD) is an important redox cofactor involved in many reactions in metabolism. The fully oxidized form, FAD, is converted to the reduced form, |FRAME: FADH2| by receiving two electrons and two protons. The reduced form can be oxidized to a |FRAME: FADH &quot;semireduced form&quot;| (semiquinone, FADH) by donating one electron and one proton. The semiquinone is then oxidized back to the fully oxidized form by losing a second electron and proton. Even though FAD has an aromatic ring system, FADH2 does not, making it significantly higher in energy. Many proteins contain a flavin moiety, either in the form of FAD or |FRAME: FMN|. They are known as |FRAME: Flavoproteins flavoproteins|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="FAD" />
